course: biology
lesson: 48
title: Life cycles and metamorphosis: frog, butterfly, ladybird
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many animals change shape completely as they grow up: a tadpole becomes a frog, a caterpillar becomes a butterfly, and this big change of body is called metamorphosis.
explain: Visit a garden pond in early spring and you may find a clump of frogspawn with hundreds of black dots; by summer some of them are froglets the size of your fingernail climbing out onto the grass.
explain: A butterfly goes through four stages, egg, caterpillar, chrysalis and adult, and inside the still chrysalis most of the caterpillar breaks down into a kind of soup that is built into a butterfly.
explain: Cartoons show a caterpillar munching for a week, then popping out as a butterfly with a ribbon, and a ladybird with one spot for every birthday; real life cycles take longer, and spots never count years.
fact: A female common frog lays about 1,000 to 2,000 eggs in a jelly clump, and because most eggs and tadpoles are eaten, often only a handful become adult frogs; toads lay their eggs in long strings instead.
fact: Tadpoles hatch after about 1 to 3 weeks, breathe with gills and swim with a tail, and grow faster in warmer water.
fact: A tadpole grows back legs first, then front legs; its gills are replaced by lungs, and its tail is not dropped but slowly taken apart and reused by the body.
fact: About 12 to 16 weeks after the eggs were laid, tiny froglets leave the water; they take two or three years to become adult frogs and can also breathe through their damp skin.
fact: A butterfly has four stages, egg, caterpillar, chrysalis and adult; peacock and red admiral butterflies lay eggs on nettles, the food their caterpillars eat, and a caterpillar moults about 4 or 5 times as it grows.
fact: Inside the chrysalis most of the caterpillar breaks down into a soup, and groups of cells waiting since it hatched build wings, legs, eyes and antennae from it.
fact: A new butterfly pumps fluid into its crumpled wings until they open and dry; the red drop below it is meconium, leftover waste, not blood.
fact: Monarch butterflies from Canada and the USA fly up to about 4,000 km to a few mountain forests in Mexico for the winter, and the trip back north takes several generations; a first-timer finds the way with the Sun and an inner clock, though it has never been there.
fact: Ladybirds also go through egg, larva, pupa and adult; their spiky grey larvae look like tiny alligators and eat aphids, and one ladybird can eat thousands of aphids in its life.
fact: A ladybird's spots show its kind, not its age: a seven-spot ladybird always has about 7 spots, and the bright colours warn birds that it tastes bad.
fact: Mayflies live a year or more as nymphs under water, but adults live only a few hours to a day or two and have no working mouth: an adult only mates and lays eggs.
fact: Grasshoppers have no caterpillar or pupa stage: wingless young called nymphs moult several times and grow wings at the last moult, which is called incomplete metamorphosis.
exercise: What is metamorphosis? | A big change of body shape as an animal grows up | An animal moving away to another country for the winter | A plant growing from a seed | An animal sleeping through the winter
exercise: How many eggs does a common frog lay in one clump? | About 1,000 to 2,000 | About 10, one at a time | Exactly 100, never more or less | About a million or more
exercise: Why does a frog lay so many eggs? | Most eggs and tadpoles are eaten, so only a few survive | Every egg becomes a frog, so the frogs can fill up every pond | It cannot count how many it lays | Big clumps are warmer to sit on
exercise: How does a young tadpole breathe? | With gills, like a fish | With lungs, like a grown-up frog | Through a straw-like tube | It does not breathe until it is a frog
exercise: Which legs does a tadpole grow first? | The back legs | The front legs | All four at once | None, it never grows any legs
exercise: What happens to a tadpole's tail? | The body slowly takes it apart and reuses it | It drops off one night and floats away in the pond | It turns into the back legs | It stays for the frog's whole life
exercise: How long after the eggs are laid do froglets leave the water? | About 12 to 16 weeks | About 2 days | About 2 or 3 years | About 1 week
exercise: What are the four stages of a butterfly's life? | Egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, adult | Egg, tadpole, froglet, adult butterfly | Seed, bud, flower, fruit | Egg, chick, nymph, adult
exercise: Why do peacock butterflies lay eggs on nettles? | Their caterpillars eat nettles, so they hatch right on their food | Nettles sting the eggs to keep them warm | Nettles hide the eggs from the rain | Any plant would do just as well, nettles just happen to be very common
exercise: What happens inside a chrysalis? | Most of the caterpillar breaks down and is rebuilt into a butterfly | The caterpillar sleeps all winter and wakes up just the same as before | The caterpillar just grows wings on its back | The caterpillar lays an egg that becomes a butterfly
exercise: What is the red drop below a butterfly that has just come out? | Leftover waste from the chrysalis, not blood | Blood from a wing that tore as it squeezed out | Juice from a flower | Paint from the twig
exercise: Where do monarch butterflies from Canada spend the winter? | In a few mountain forests in Mexico | Asleep in burrows under the snow in Canada | In the Arctic, where it is dark all day | In the hot, dry deserts of central Australia
exercise: What does a ladybird larva look like, and what does it eat? | A small spiky grey larva like a tiny alligator, eating aphids | A tiny red beetle with no spots yet, eating the leaves of roses | A green caterpillar, eating roses | A white worm, eating soil
exercise: What do the spots on a ladybird tell you? | What kind of ladybird it is | How old it is, one spot for each year | How many babies it has had so far | How many aphids it has eaten
exercise: How long does an adult mayfly live? | A few hours to a day or two | Exactly one month, no more | One whole year | Ten years or more
exercise: How does a young grasshopper grow up? | It looks like a small wingless adult and moults until it has wings | It is a caterpillar first, then it spins a chrysalis and changes inside | It hatches from frogspawn | It hatches already with full wings
test: «my ladybird's got 7 spots so she's 7 years old» What is right? | The spots show the kind of ladybird, not its age | Right, she gets one new spot for each birthday, mate | Right, but only for girls | No, she is 7 months old
test: In a cartoon a caterpillar goes into a cocoon, and the very same caterpillar crawls out with wings glued on. What really happens? | Most of its body breaks down and a butterfly is built from it | The caterpillar just grows two pairs of wings on its back | The caterpillar swaps places with a butterfly | Nothing changes inside, it only gets a new skin and a pair of wings
test: «there's blood under the new butterfly, it's hurt!!» What is right? | It is meconium, leftover waste, and the butterfly is fine | It is badly hurt and will probably die before it can fly | It is red nectar it drank | It is the butterfly's tears
test: «saw a tiny spiky alligator thing eating greenfly on the roses, kill it?» What is the best answer? | No, it is a ladybird larva and it eats the aphids | Yes, it is a pest that will eat all your roses | Yes, it is a baby lizard | No, it is a baby caterpillar
test: What would happen to tadpoles in a pond kept in deep shade instead of sun? | They would grow more slowly and become froglets later | They would grow faster because they stay cool | They would turn into toads | They would never hatch at all
test: «tadpoles' tails just fall off one day, saw it on telly» What is right? | The tail shrinks as the body takes it apart and reuses it | Right, it drops off in one go | Right, other tadpoles bite it off | The tail slowly turns into the frog's long sticky tongue
test: «why do mayflies even bother, they live like a day» What is the clever answer? | They live a year or more as nymphs; the adult only mates and lays eggs | They die because they forget to eat | They live only one day in total, from the egg to the end | They are all eaten by fish on their very first day of life
test: A monarch butterfly arrives in Mexico for the first time. How did it find the way? | Using the Sun and an inner clock, though it has never been there | Its parents flew with it last year and showed it the way | It followed a map left by other butterflies | It asked the birds on the way
test: In a game, a grasshopper spins a chrysalis before it gets wings. What is wrong? | Grasshoppers have no pupa: nymphs moult and get wings at the end | Nothing, that is right | Grasshoppers hatch from frogspawn | Grasshoppers never get wings and only ever hop about in the grass
test: «the polluted stream's fine for frogs, they breathe air» What is right? | Frogs also breathe through their skin, so dirty water harms them | Right, frogs only breathe through their lungs, so water can't hurt them | Right, pollution makes frogs stronger | Frogs only live in the sea
test: «found 2000 frog eggs, so 2000 frogs next year, sweet» What is more likely? | Only a handful will become adult frogs | All 2000 will become frogs, if it rains | Exactly 1000 will survive | None ever survive
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises metamorphosis? | A marked change in the form of the body during an animal's development | The seasonal migration of an animal to a warmer region for the purpose of breeding | The dormancy of an animal throughout the winter | The growth of a plant from a seed
exam: Identify the correct order of the stages in the life of a butterfly. | Egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, adult | Caterpillar, egg, adult, chrysalis | Egg, chrysalis, caterpillar, adult | Chrysalis, egg, caterpillar, adult
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the spots of the ladybird. | The number of spots marks the species and does not vary with age | One spot is acquired upon each anniversary of the hatching of the insect | The spots are lost progressively as the insect ages | The number of spots indicates the quantity of aphids consumed
exam: Ascertain the manner in which the grasshopper attains its adult form. | By successive moults, the wingless nymph acquiring wings at the last | By the formation of a chrysalis in which its body is rebuilt | By hatching from its egg in its complete adult form, wings included | By the development of a tadpole into an adult upon land
